A||||2||GeeDee86GT||Engine Heat Shielding????||||5||
Z||000000||GeeDee86GT||01-26-2001||01:07 PM||||I'm soon to drop the cradle for a clutch & exhaust manifold leak repair. While it's empty I'd like to do some rust repair in the engine compartment. How much of the heat insulation can be removed without causing a safety concern? Also can some of the tin shields be ditched?  I'm considering using an 84 style hood grate in the center to let some heat out, and using a small tonneau cover on it for rainy days. Comments?<BR>Gary||||reg||5
Z||000001||My7Fieros||01-26-2001||03:28 PM||||Assuming your vehicle is a notchback, you can use the 84 style hood...prob wouldnt hurt. As far as those tin shields, Id leave them in place. They are there for a reason...namely to prevent oil spilage from making contact with an exhaust component. If you've ever taken them off, you'd see. I'd leave them on.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://tgowens.com/My7Fieros_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mark||01-26-2001||04:22 PM||||I agree with My7,  GM put the metal heat shielding in there for very good reason.  As far as the insulation, I would NOT remove the stuff from the firewall either.  The side insulation however is apparently there for sound deadening.  Several people on the forum have removed the side insulation without problems.  ||||reg||1
